FEATURE: component-teardown
DATE: 2026-09-17
SCOPE: give every component a destroy(), and make the core's delegated listeners detachable
FOLLOWS: changelog/resizer-docs

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected. There is no build step
and no dependency graph to propagate through; changes take effect on the next page
load.

Consumer-visible surface:
  - $$.attachEventHandler()   now returns a handle; previously returned undefined
  - 14 component files        gained a destroy() method
  - vUX-carousel.js           now imports TouchHandler (bug fix, see below)
  - vUX-formValidator.js      loader insertion order corrected (bug fix, see below)

The public API is additive. No member was renamed or removed, and no existing call
signature changed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before this change destroy() existed on exactly two of sixteen components
(DataView and Resizer). Everything else bound listeners, started intervals and
injected DOM with no way to undo any of it. For a plain multi-page site that is
invisible: the page unloads and the browser reclaims everything. It is not
invisible under SPAEngine, where route changes tear content out of a live document
and every listener bound to window or document survives the swap.

This also blocks the planned BYOC component module: a consumer component that
nests vUX components needs something to call when it is unmounted.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE CORE CHANGE THAT HAD TO COME FIRST
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

$$.attachEventHandler() delegated to a listener bound on window and kept no
reference to the closure it built, so nothing could ever be handed to
removeEventListener(). Six components used it. It now returns { detach } and the
callers store the handle. Older calls that ignore the return value are unaffected.

vUX-resizer.js had already worked around this by binding its own named listeners;
the comment at its addEventHanler() records why.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT DESTROY() DOES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Uniformly: detach every listener the component bound, clear every timer and
animation it started, and remove the DOM it injected. Where the component mutated
consumer markup and retained enough to reverse it, it reverses it — ToolTip gives
'title' attributes back, TimeLineList strips its marker classes, Carousel clears
the attributes it wrote onto the sliders.

It does not attempt to rebuild markup that was replaced in place without the
original being kept. That is the case for the FormComponents widgets, which
substitute custom markup for the consumer's native inputs. There, destroy() unbinds
and says so in a comment rather than reconstructing an approximation of an element
the consumer did not write.

Two shared resources are left alone on purpose, in both cases because they belong
to more than one instance:
  - the 'carouselStyles' sheet, whose data-id every Carousel shares
  - a FormValidator's 'config.modal', which is supplied by the consumer

One shared resource is now reference counted instead of leaked: ModalDisplayer's
four document-level handlers are detached by the last instance to be destroyed.

Re-initialization after destroy() throws, following the message DataView already
used. Resizer is the exception: its destroy() predates this work, guards on
'initialized' rather than a destroyed flag, and deliberately allows reuse. That was
left as it was rather than narrowed under a shipped component's feet.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TWO PRE-EXISTING BUGS FOUND WHILE DOING THIS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Both were found by running the components rather than by reading them, and both
are on default configuration paths.

1. Carousel never imported TouchHandler, although it constructs one whenever
   config.touchResponse is true — which is the default. initialize() threw
   'TouchHandler is not defined' on any page that did not explicitly disable touch.

2. FormValidator.createLoader() centred the loader while it was still detached.
   $$.sm().center() reads parentNode.scrollHeight on its static-element branch, and
   a detached element computes 'position: static', so initialize() threw
   'Cannot read properties of null' under the default configuration. The loader is
   now inserted before it is centred, and given an inline position so that center()
   does not depend on the asynchronously linked stylesheet having arrived.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every source file was syntax checked. Beyond that, each component was booted
against a DOM, initialized and destroyed, with window and document listener
registration instrumented so that a listener surviving destroy() fails the check.

Covered: ToolTip, TimeLineList, TouchHandler, Carousel, ListScroller, AutoWriter,
DOMDrawer, ModalDisplayer, ProgressIndicator, FormValidator, DataView, and the
select and checkbox widgets of FormComponents. All pass with zero listeners
surviving. Both bugs above were found by this pass.

Not exercised at runtime: SPAEngine (needs a route config and a server), and the
datePicker, radio, slideSwitch and file widgets, whose destroy() is the same
mechanism as the two widgets that are covered.
